overlay_vulkan: give the output the main input's time base

The output link kept no time base of its own, and framesync worked in the
finest time base of its two inputs, which every picture leaving the filter
then carried. When the main picture and the overlay do not share a time base
(a film and its subtitle, always), the timestamps came out many times too
large and a player placed nothing: measured with a film in 1/1000 and a
subtitle in 1/90000, the second picture came out at 3.6 seconds rather than
0.04. Both are now the main input's, as overlay_vaapi sets them.

--- a/libavfilter/vf_overlay_vulkan.c
+++ b/libavfilter/vf_overlay_vulkan.c
@@ -190,6 +190,11 @@
     if (err < 0)
         return err;
 
+    /* The pictures that come out carry the timestamps framesync gives them,
+     * in its own time base: both are made the main input's, as overlay_vaapi
+     * does. */
+    s->fs.time_base = outlink->time_base = avctx->inputs[0]->time_base;
+
     return ff_framesync_configure(&s->fs);
 }
 
